module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.single_arm_sum_parses_as_an_alias_and_becomes_unreadable

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }

data single_arm_sum_parses_as_an_alias_and_becomes_unreadable: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "single_arm_sum_parses_as_an_alias_and_becomes_unreadable" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "single-arm sum parses as an alias and becomes unreadable. INVALID STATE: an author writes a one-member roster as `type K = A`, intending a sum with one arm, and the ARM COUNT SILENTLY DECIDES WHAT KIND OF DECLARATION IT IS -- one arm parses as a TYPE ALIAS, two or more as a coproduct. HARM: the alias reaches a consumer as a bare nominal node carrying NO resolved target and NO children, so any consumer that reads a declaration's members reads nothing, and there is no diagnostic anywhere saying the declaration is not the shape that was written.",

    "RECEIPT, 2026-09-20, session gentle-seal-490, printed by the refusal itself rather than inferred. A kind annotation `type MachineWidth<bits: WidthResolution>` was given a one-arm roster, `type WidthResolution = PointerWidth`. The wall that checks kind inhabitance then refused the kind's OWN declared inhabitant, twice, on the real corpus, and the self-diagnosing message carried the cause: `'PointerWidth [decl=WidthResolution inferred=absent resolved_target= children=0]'`. Changing the roster to two arms (`= StaticWidthIndex | PointerWidth`) made the same declaration a coproduct with readable children and the same check correct.",

    "THE SECOND HALF, because the obvious rescue does not work: resolving the declaration instead of reading it does NOT recover the target. The kind node a parser synthesizes for an annotation carries no `ident`, and lookup_type_for keys on `node.ident`, so node-keyed resolution returns the node unchanged. Both the read route and the resolve route fail, for two different reasons, and neither reports anything.",

    "WHY READING THE SOURCE DID NOT PREDICT IT, which is the part worth carrying: v1.compiler.infer local_binding_for_item's alias arm carries `inferred: item.inferred`, and v1.compiler.infer_env lookup_type_by_name returns `binding.resolved`. BOTH STATEMENTS ARE TRUE. Neither predicts this call site, because the single-arm parse has already made the declaration an alias before either applies. TRUE-BUT-NOT-PREDICTIVE is the failure, not a misreading, and it cost three build cycles before an instrument built into the refusal settled it in one. A related note already existed saying a single-arm coproduct is a type alias; it was filed against breakage at the IMPORT site, was read as being about that consumer, and the single-arm form was written anyway -- a note correct about the mechanism but filed under one consumer reads as covered and is worse than no note.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: below the ladder -- silent wrongness at the declaration, with the author's intent and the parsed shape diverging and nothing reporting it. ATTAINABLE CEILING: structurally guaranteed. The parser knows the arm count and the `=` form at the moment it chooses between alias and coproduct, so it can refuse a one-arm sum outright, or admit it as a one-arm coproduct, rather than silently reclassifying it. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: v1.compiler.parse distinguishes an intended one-arm sum from an alias -- SUFFICIENT FOR no declaration to change kind as a function of its arm count without a located diagnostic, so a consumer reading a roster either reads it or is told why it cannot. STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE would additionally require the surface to make the two forms unconfusable, which is a language-design question this row does not decide.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "std.machine_constraints", decl_name: "WidthResolution", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v1.compiler.infer_resolve", decl_name: "type_arg_kind_inhabitance", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
